          <a href="#application" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Apply</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Label and Safety</a>
          <a href="#lawn-health" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Lawn Habits</a>
          <a href="#which-to-buy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Which to Buy</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best crabgrass preventer is the one that is down and watered in before the soil warms, because crabgrass is an annual that comes back from seed every spring. Get a pre-emergent barrier in place before germination and most of next summer's crabgrass never appears. Miss the window and no bag on the shelf will fix it. This guide compares the products that work, by active ingredient, fertilizer and coverage, and explains the timing that matters more than the brand.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not test these products on our own lawns. The picks are researched from product labels, manufacturer pages, EPA label documents and retailer listings, and each figure is credited to its source. Where retailers listed active ingredients or concentrations differently, we said so and told you to check the bag, because the label on the bag in your hands is the legal and practical authority.
          </p>

          <p>
            We grouped the field by what actually differs between crabgrass preventers: the active ingredient, whether fertilizer is included, and whether you spread or spray it. Pendimethalin, dithiopyr and prodiamine are the three active ingredients homeowners meet most often, and each behaves a little differently in timing and residual. Within each group we favoured widely sold products with clear coverage figures.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighed timing forgiveness heavily. Most failed crabgrass programs are late programs, so products with dithiopyr, which also controls very young crabgrass after it emerges, scored well for homeowners who struggle to hit the window. We also weighed cost per square foot, which is where professional prodiamine products pull far ahead on large lawns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every product here is a pre-emergent, which means it stops crabgrass seeds from establishing rather than killing plants already growing. If crabgrass is already up and spreading in midsummer, you need a post-emergent product instead, and our guide to the <a href="/best-weed-killer/">best weed killers</a> covers those options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-preemergents-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Crabgrass Preventers Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crabgrass is an annual grass. Every plant dies with the first hard frost, and every plant next summer comes from seed dropped this year. That is why prevention works so well: a pre-emergent herbicide forms a barrier in the top layer of soil that stops germinating seedlings from developing roots, so the seed sprouts and fails before you ever see it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The barrier only works if it is in place before germination and stays intact. Watering the granules in, as most labels direct, moves the active ingredient off the grass blades and into the top of the soil. Disturbing that layer afterward, by core aerating or dethatching, breaks the barrier and lets seedlings through in the gaps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pre-emergents do nothing to crabgrass that has already germinated and grown past the seedling stage. Dithiopyr is the partial exception, with labels claiming control of crabgrass up to a few weeks after germination while the plants are still very small. Pendimethalin and prodiamine do not have that early post-emergent activity, so with those products, timing is everything.
          </p>

          <p>
            A crabgrass preventer is not a fix for a thin lawn. Crabgrass thrives where turf is sparse, mowed short or stressed by heat, so the long-term answer is a dense lawn that shades the soil. Pre-emergents buy you clean seasons while you build that density through mowing height, feeding and overseeding at the right time of year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not field-test these products. The picks are researched from product labels, manufacturer pages, EPA label documents and retailer listings, credited in each write-up. Where listings disagreed on ingredients or concentrations, we say so; always follow the label on the bag you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
