        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. When to Apply: Soil Temperature, Not the Calendar
          </h2>

          <p>
            University extension services commonly cite soil temperature as the trigger for crabgrass germination, with seeds starting to sprout once the top inch or two of soil holds around 55°F for several days in a row. The goal is to have the pre-emergent down and watered in before that happens, which in much of the country means early to mid spring, earlier in the South and later in the North.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the date shifts every year, soil temperature is a better guide than the calendar. Several extension services publish soil temperature maps, and a simple soil thermometer pushed two inches into the lawn in the morning gives you a local reading. Many gardeners also use the old cue of forsythia blooms, which tend to appear around the same time soils warm, as a reminder to get product down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Applying too early carries its own risk. Pre-emergents break down in the soil over weeks to months, so a very early application can run out before the end of the crabgrass germination season, which continues into early summer. That is why many labels allow, and many lawn professionals favour, a split application: half the annual rate in early spring and the rest about six to eight weeks later.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you missed the window, dithiopyr products like the Jonathan Green and Preen picks give a few weeks of grace on very young plants. Beyond that, switch to spot-treating crabgrass with a post-emergent and plan to apply on time next spring. Our guide on <a href="/when-to-fertilize-lawn/">when to fertilize your lawn</a> covers how this timing fits the rest of the year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="active-ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pendimethalin vs Dithiopyr vs Prodiamine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pendimethalin is the active ingredient in Scotts Halts and Step 1 and in many store-brand preventers. It has a long track record and works well when applied before germination, but it offers no control of crabgrass that has already emerged. Its residual is typically shorter than prodiamine's, so in long seasons a second application may be needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dithiopyr, sold under the Dimension brand and used in the Jonathan Green, Preen and GreenView picks, is the most forgiving. Labels claim it also controls crabgrass seedlings for a few weeks after they emerge, which covers the common mistake of applying a little late. It can also be used in some lawns where pendimethalin is less suitable, so check the label for your grass type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prodiamine, the active ingredient in Barricade and in the generic Prodiamine 65 WDG, is known for long residual activity at low use rates, which is why professionals rely on it for split programs. It is mostly sold as a concentrate you spray or as an ingredient in professional granular blends, and like pendimethalin it has no post-emergent activity.
          </p>

          <p>
            All three will stop grass seed from establishing for as long as they remain active, and all three bind tightly to soil and organic matter. Whichever you choose, follow the label rate for your grass type and region; using more does not make the barrier stronger, it just raises cost and the risk of injuring the turf or nearby plants.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overseeding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Crabgrass Preventers and Grass Seed
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common conflict in spring lawn care is wanting to prevent crabgrass and seed bare spots at the same time. Standard pre-emergents cannot tell crabgrass seed from lawn seed, so a lawn treated with pendimethalin, dithiopyr or prodiamine will not establish new grass from seed until the product has broken down, which can take months depending on the product and rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual solution is to separate the jobs by season. For cool-season grasses, extension services widely recommend overseeding in late summer or early fall, when soil is warm, air is cooler and crabgrass is not germinating. Use the crabgrass preventer in spring and seed in the fall, and the two never compete.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you must seed in spring, some starter products use mesotrione, an active ingredient labelled for use at seeding because it suppresses many weeds without stopping new cool-season grass. Products marketed as starter fertilizer with weed preventer often use it. Check the label carefully, since the pre-emergents in this guide are not those products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the waiting period on the bag before you seed anywhere you have applied a preventer, and keep the product out of bare patches you plan to seed this spring by skipping them with the spreader. Our guide to the <a href="/best-grass-seed/">best grass seed</a> covers varieties and seeding rates for whichever season you choose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Apply It Evenly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Granular preventers go on with a broadcast or drop spreader set to the rate on the bag. A broadcast spreader is faster and less likely to leave stripes, while a drop spreader is more precise along beds and edges. Our guide to the <a href="/best-broadcast-spreader/">best broadcast spreaders</a> covers both, including models with edge guards that keep granules out of beds.
          </p>

          <p>
            For even coverage, many lawn professionals apply half the rate in one direction and the other half at right angles, which fills the gaps a single pass leaves. Overlap passes slightly according to your spreader's instructions, and close the hopper when you stop or turn so you do not dump a heavy patch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweep or blow any granules off driveways, sidewalks and patios back onto the lawn. Granules left on hard surfaces can wash into storm drains, and some products contain iron or other ingredients that can stain concrete. Most labels then call for watering the product in with about half an inch of water, either from irrigation or a well-timed rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sprayable products like Prodiamine 65 WDG are measured by weight, mixed in a pump or backpack sprayer and applied at a calibrated rate per 1,000 square feet. Calibrate the sprayer first by spraying a measured area with plain water, so you know how much mix you apply per square foot, then mix the concentrate to match. A marking dye helps you see where you have been.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Label, Safety and Environmental Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every herbicide label is a legal document, and the directions on it are the ones to follow. That includes the rate, the grasses it is labelled for, the maximum number of applications per year, the re-entry interval and any restrictions near water. If anything here conflicts with the label on your product, the label wins.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep children and pets off the treated lawn for the time the label specifies, usually until the product has been watered in and the grass has dried. Store products in their original containers, out of reach and away from fertilizers and seed, and wear the gloves and other protection the label asks for during mixing and application.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep granules and spray away from waterways, storm drains and ponds. Several states and localities restrict fertilizer applications, particularly phosphorus and applications near water or before heavy rain, and some limit fertilizer in certain seasons. If you use a product combined with fertilizer, those rules apply to it too, so check local regulations before spreading.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some ornamental plants, trees and shrubs can be injured by turf herbicides, especially the broadleaf herbicides in weed-and-feed products. Keep weed-and-feed off garden beds, avoid applying over tree root zones the label warns about, and do not use clippings from a recently treated lawn as mulch around vegetables unless the label says it is safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lawn-health" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. The Lawn Habits That Keep Crabgrass Away
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dense lawn is the best crabgrass preventer, because crabgrass seed needs light and warm soil to germinate. Mowing cool-season grass at the higher end of its recommended range, often 3 to 4 inches, shades the soil surface and keeps it cooler, and extension services consistently list mowing height as one of the strongest controls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water deeply and less often rather than a little every day. Frequent shallow watering keeps the soil surface moist and warm, which is exactly what crabgrass seed wants, while deep, infrequent watering encourages turf roots to go down. Early morning watering also reduces disease pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feed the lawn at the right time for its grass type. Cool-season lawns benefit most from fall feeding, which builds roots and density that crowd out spring weeds, while warm-season lawns are fed in late spring and summer. Combine that with aeration at the right time, ideally in the fall for cool-season grass, and our guide on <a href="/when-to-aerate-lawn/">when to aerate your lawn</a> explains why aerating after a spring pre-emergent breaks the barrier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to the places crabgrass shows up first: the hot strip along driveways and sidewalks, thin spots and areas scalped by the mower. Those edges heat up earliest and are where germination starts. Some homeowners give those edges a slightly earlier application or extra attention every year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Crabgrass Preventer Should You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For an established lawn with a history of crabgrass and no seeding planned, Scotts Turf Builder with Halts is the simplest choice: pendimethalin plus a spring feeding, sold everywhere, applied through any spreader. If you follow a set annual schedule, Scotts Step 1 does the same job as the start of a four-step program.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you tend to apply late, or spring warms up unpredictably where you live, choose a dithiopyr product. Jonathan Green's Veri-Green Plus includes fertilizer, Preen Lawn Crabgrass Control leaves it out for lawns fed in the fall, and GreenView's 3-in-1 adds broadleaf weed control for lawns that also have dandelions and clover.
          </p>

          <p>
            For large lawns and owners comfortable mixing and spraying, Prodiamine 65 WDG costs far less per square foot than any bagged granular product and offers long residual activity that suits a split spring application. It rewards the effort with the most control per dollar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, the result depends far more on timing and even coverage than on the brand. Get the product down before soil reaches about 55°F, water it in, leave the soil undisturbed, and keep building a dense lawn. Pair this with a good <a href="/best-fertilizer-for-grass/">lawn fertilizer</a> program and crabgrass becomes a minor annual task rather than a summer-long fight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying After Crabgrass Is Up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pre-emergents stop seeds from establishing; they do not kill established plants. Get the product down before soil reaches about 55°F, or use a dithiopyr product if you are late, and spot-treat anything already growing with a post-emergent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Seeding Into a Treated Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Crabgrass preventers stop grass seed too. Do not overseed where you have applied a standard pre-emergent this spring. Seed cool-season lawns in early fall instead, or use a starter product labelled for use at seeding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Aerating After Applying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Core aeration and dethatching break the herbicide barrier in the top layer of soil and let crabgrass through the holes. Aerate in the fall, or before you apply, never in the weeks after a spring pre-emergent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Watering-In</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most labels call for about half an inch of water to move the active ingredient off the grass blades and into the soil. Granules left sitting on dry leaves can break down or blow away before they form the barrier.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using More Than the Label Rate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavier application does not make a stronger barrier. It raises cost, risks turf and plant injury, and can exceed the annual maximum on the label. Calibrate your spreader and follow the rate for your grass type.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

